exports[`drop prompt > is deterministic and matches its snapshots > detailed-existing-experienced 1`] = `
"# Build: Ink-ball pile

## What I want
Balls in three inks fall in and pile up at the bottom, their colors overprinting where they overlap. Tapping drops another ball where I tap. Moving through the pile shoves it around and it settles again.

## Look
- Colors ("Cherry Cola" palette): background #2A0A0E, text #FFF1E6, secondary text #E3B5AC, accents #E8203A, #FFB38A, #FFE9C7.
- Where accent colors overlap, add them so they glow (canvas globalCompositeOperation "lighter").
- Font: "Anybody" from Google Fonts (variable width 50–150, weight 100–900), loaded with https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Anybody:ital,wdth,wght@0,50..150,100..900;1,50..150,100..900&display=swap, with a system sans-serif fallback.
- Size the canvas for devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and do all math in CSS pixels.
- Draw balls as circles in their accent color, one path per accent (skipping any still above the top), using the blend mode above.

## How it moves
- A full-screen canvas (100svh, at least 540px), transparent, cleared every frame. At the start, round(clamp(width ÷ 40, 14, 38)) balls are made, each at x = rmax + random × (width − 2 × rmax) and y = −random × height × 1.4 − rmax (Math.random() in that order, before the ball's own random calls), so they rain in.
- There are at most 90 balls; adding one more removes the oldest.
- Ball radii run from rmin = clamp(short side × 3%, 12, 26) px to rmax = clamp(short side × 7.5%, 26, 70) px. Making a ball calls Math.random() for its radius = rmin + (rmax − rmin) × random^1.7 (mostly small balls), then for its starting sideways speed = (random × 2 − 1) × 1.5; its mass is radius², and balls take the accents in turn (ball k uses accent k mod 3, counting every ball ever made).
- Each frame runs 3 substeps with h = f ÷ 3. In each, for every ball in order: vy += 0.42 × h; vx ×= 0.996^h; x += vx × h; y += vy × h; side walls at 0 and the width put it back and bounce it with 0.5 of its sideways speed; the floor sits at height × 0.94: a ball past it is put back on it, its vy becomes −|vy| × floor bounce, and vx ×= 0.97^h. Then the cursor push (below). Then every pair (i < j, in order) that overlaps is pushed apart along the line between centers by the overlap, split by inverse mass, and if they move toward each other gets an impulse jn = −((1 + ball bounce) × relative speed along the line) ÷ (1/m₁ + 1/m₂).
- Floor bounce 0.3.
- Ball bounce 0.25.
- The pointer is an invisible ball of radius clamp(short side × 9%, 44, 90) px with the pointer's velocity (its movement since last frame ÷ f): a ball overlapping it is placed just outside it, and if it moves toward the pointer (relative to the pointer's velocity) its velocity −= 1.5 × that speed along the line. A mouse pushes whenever it is over the canvas, a finger only after holding still for 0.25s (so swipes still scroll). A click or tap (on release) adds a ball at that point with vy = −3.
- Run one requestAnimationFrame loop. dt = seconds since the last frame, clamped to 0.05; f = min(dt × 60, 2.5).
- On resize, recompute the sizes and floor, and keep each ball inside the side walls. The starting balls are made once, on the first resize.
- Reduced-motion still frame: the settled pile (run 300 frames of the physics with f = 1 and no pointer before drawing).

## How people interact
- The canvas has touch-action: pan-y.

## Where this goes
First, look at my project's files and figure out what framework and styling it uses. Add this as a component that fits how the project is already built. Don't change unrelated files. When you finish, tell me which files you added or changed and how to put the component on a page.

## Rules
- Smooth on a mid-range laptop and a phone: one requestAnimationFrame loop, batched canvas drawing, devicePixelRatio capped at 2, pause when off-screen.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion: start paused and show a meaningful still frame, with a visible Play/Pause button.
- Accessible: real buttons, visible keyboard focus, text contrast of at least 4.5:1, decorative canvases hidden from screen readers.
- Works on touch screens without blocking vertical scrolling.

## When you're done
Keep the explanation short: list the files and the key parameters I can tweak."
`;

