exports[`filings prompt > is deterministic and matches its snapshots > detailed-existing-experienced 1`] = `
"# Build: Iron filings

## What I want
Thousands of tiny iron filings scattered across the screen. A magnet follows my cursor and the filings turn to trace its field lines, glowing brighter near the poles. Clicking leaves a magnet behind, so I can build up a pattern with a few magnets.

## Look
- Colors ("Toxic" palette): background #10140A, text #F1FFD9, secondary text #B5C99A, accents #B6FF1A, #FF6A00, #9B5CFF.
- Where accent colors overlap, add them so they glow (canvas globalCompositeOperation "lighter").
- Font: "Anybody" from Google Fonts (variable width 50–150, weight 100–900), loaded with https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Anybody:ital,wdth,wght@0,50..150,100..900;1,50..150,100..900&display=swap, with a system sans-serif fallback.
- A full-screen canvas (100svh, at least 540px), transparent over the background and cleared every frame. Size it for devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and do all math in CSS pixels.

## How it moves
- Scatter 3000 filings (1200 on screens under 700px wide) at random: Math.random() for x, y, then the starting angle = random × π.
- Draw each filing as a line 8px long, centered on its spot and turned to its angle, 1.2px wide with round caps, colored by the pull it feels (s, below): the soft text color below 0.12, the third accent below 0.6, the first accent from there up; one path per color, using the blend mode above. Then draw the magnets on top: a bar is a thick line 10px wide with round caps from its center to its north pole in the second accent and from its center to its south pole in the third; a single pole is a dot of radius 10 ÷ 2 in the second accent.
- At each filing add up the field of every pole: a north pole adds (filing − pole) ÷ r³ and a south pole adds (pole − filing) ÷ r³, where r = √(distance² + 8²). The pull the filing feels is s = min(1, |field| × 260² × 1).
- Each frame, turn each filing toward the field's direction: a filing has no front or back, so take the difference between the field's angle and the filing's angle, wrapped into −π/2 … π/2, and add it × 0.15 × s × f to the filing's angle.
- Each magnet is a bar: its north pole 70 ÷ 2 px from its center along its angle, its south pole the same distance the other way.
- One magnet follows the pointer (it starts in the middle, pointing right, and stays where the pointer left it); each frame it turns toward the direction it is moving (if faster than 0.5px per frame) by the wrapped angle difference × 0.08 × f. A click or tap (on release) leaves a copy of it behind at that point with its current angle; at most 3 copies stay, and the oldest is removed first.
- Run one requestAnimationFrame loop. dt = seconds since the last frame, clamped to 0.05; f = min(dt × 60, 2.5).
- Scatter the filings again on every resize; magnets stay.
- Reduced-motion still frame: only the cursor magnet, in the middle, pointing right, after 120 frames of turning with f = 1.

## How people interact
- The canvas has touch-action: pan-y, so vertical swipes still scroll.

## Where this goes
First, look at my project's files and figure out what framework and styling it uses. Add this as a component that fits how the project is already built. Don't change unrelated files. When you finish, tell me which files you added or changed and how to put the component on a page.

## Rules
- Smooth on a mid-range laptop and a phone: one requestAnimationFrame loop, batched canvas drawing, devicePixelRatio capped at 2, pause when off-screen.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion: start paused and show a meaningful still frame, with a visible Play/Pause button.
- Accessible: real buttons, visible keyboard focus, text contrast of at least 4.5:1, decorative canvases hidden from screen readers.
- Works on touch screens without blocking vertical scrolling.

## When you're done
Keep the explanation short: list the files and the key parameters I can tweak."
`;

