"# Build: Fireworks

## What I want
Fireworks across the whole screen. Tapping launches a rocket that climbs to where I tapped, slows down and bursts into colored sparks with fading trails, which droop and fade as they fall. A rocket also goes up by itself every few seconds so it never looks empty.

## Look
- Colors ("Golden Hour" palette): background #FFE8CC, text #3A1A0A, secondary text #7A4A2A, accents #FF8A3D, #D9467A, #7B5CD6.
- Where accent colors overlap, multiply them like ink on paper (canvas globalCompositeOperation "multiply").
- Font: "Anybody" from Google Fonts (variable width 50–150, weight 100–900), loaded with https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Anybody:ital,wdth,wght@0,50..150,100..900;1,50..150,100..900&display=swap, with a system sans-serif fallback.
- A full-screen canvas (100svh, at least 540px), transparent over the background and cleared every frame. Size it for devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and do all math in CSS pixels.

## How it moves
- Each burst makes 160 sparks (80 on screens under 700px wide), all starting at the burst point.
- Each burst first calls Math.random() to pick its shape (below 1/3 sphere, below 2/3 ring, otherwise willow): a sphere (per spark, call Math.random() for an angle a = random × 2π, then u = random × 2 − 1; speed = 6 × √(1 − u²) px per frame in direction a, so sparks crowd toward the rim like a ball seen from the side); a ring (one Math.random() call turns the whole ring by random × 2π; spark i flies at angle turn + i ÷ count × 2π at speed 6, its vertical speed × 0.6 so the ring looks tilted); or a willow (spread like the sphere, but its sparks use air drag 0.94 and live 1.8 × as long, so they droop and hang).
- Each spark remembers its last 8 positions (one per frame, recorded before it moves). Draw it as one line through them to its current position, 1.8px wide with round caps and joins. A rocket is a line of the same width from where it was 8 ÷ 60 seconds earlier on its rise curve to where it is.
- Every frame, for each spark: velocity ×= its drag^f; vertical velocity += 0.06 × f; position += velocity × f.
- Sparks have air drag 0.975 and live 1.6s; a burst's sparks all fade together: opacity = 1 − (age ÷ life)², and the burst is removed at the end of its life.
- A click or tap (on release) queues a rocket aimed at that point (at most 3 waiting; more are ignored). A queued rocket launches once 0.35s have passed since the last launch. When the queue is empty and 3s have passed since the last launch (the first time: 0.4s after the start), queue an automatic rocket: x = (0.15 + random × (1 − 2 × 0.15)) × width, then y = (0.15 + random × (0.45 − 0.15)) × height.
- A rocket starts at the bottom edge below its target and its height follows an ease-out cubic (1 − (1 − u)³) from the bottom to the target, u = its age ÷ 1.1s capped at 1. Its color is one of the three accents (one Math.random() call at launch: floor(random × 3)). When it arrives it bursts there in its color, but never within 0.35s of the previous burst: until then it waits at the top.
- For 0.5s after it bursts, a burst lights up only the area around itself: a radial gradient circle of radius 90px from its color to transparent, at opacity 0.22 × (1 − age ÷ 0.5). Draw glows first, then rockets, then sparks (each burst as one path at its opacity), all with the blend mode above.
- Run one requestAnimationFrame loop. dt = seconds since the last frame, clamped to 0.05; f = min(dt × 60, 2.5); t = seconds the loop has run (the sum of dt).
- Each frame, in this order: queue an automatic rocket if one is due, launch a queued rocket if allowed, move rockets and burst the ones that arrived, move sparks, draw.
- Safety: at most 3 bursts start in any one second, however fast I tap; nothing lights up the whole screen and the background never flashes.
- Reduced-motion still frame: three bursts (sphere, ring, willow, in the three accents) at 25%/34%, 50%/20% and 75%/40% of the width/height, 30 frames after they burst, with no rockets.

## How people interact
- The canvas has touch-action: pan-y, so swipes still scroll.

## Where this goes
Write this as a single React component file (.jsx, or .tsx if my project uses TypeScript) with no extra libraries. Include a short usage example.

## Rules
- Smooth on a mid-range laptop and a phone: one requestAnimationFrame loop, batched canvas drawing, devicePixelRatio capped at 2, pause when off-screen.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion: start paused and show a meaningful still frame, with a visible Play/Pause button.
- Accessible: real buttons, visible keyboard focus, text contrast of at least 4.5:1, decorative canvases hidden from screen readers.
- Works on touch screens without blocking vertical scrolling.

## When you're done
Keep the explanation short: list the files and the key parameters I can tweak."
`;

