exports[`overprint prompt > is deterministic and matches its snapshots > detailed-existing-experienced 1`] = `
"# Build: Overprint blobs

## What I want
Three big ink blobs that chase my cursor, each on a spring of a different speed, arranged in a slowly turning triangle. Where they overlap the inks mix into new colors. Fast movement stretches them in the direction they travel, a click makes them jump in a quick staggered pulse, and when I leave them alone they wander.

## Look
- Colors ("Riso" palette): background #F4F2FF, text #1A1240, secondary text #4E4777, accents #EC1C7F, #2A47EA, #FF7A00.
- Where accent colors overlap, multiply them like ink on paper (canvas globalCompositeOperation "multiply").
- Font: "Anybody" from Google Fonts (variable width 50–150, weight 100–900), loaded with https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Anybody:ital,wdth,wght@0,50..150,100..900;1,50..150,100..900&display=swap, with a system sans-serif fallback.
- Size the canvas for devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and do all math in CSS pixels.

## How it moves
- A full-screen canvas (100svh, at least 540px), transparent, cleared every frame, with three ink blobs (i = 0, 1, 2, in accents 1, 2 and 3). R = short side × 21%. Blob i's radius is R × its scale × (1 − i × 0.04). Draw each blob as a closed path of 72 points (j = 0 … 72) filled in its accent, using the blend mode above, in the order 0, 1, 2.
- Point j sits at angle θ = j ÷ 72 × 2π; with w = θ + the blob's heading, its radius is r0 × (1 + 0.055 × sin(3w + t × 1.3 + 2i) + 0.035 × sin(5w − t × 0.9 + i)).
- A moving blob stretches: with speed = its velocity's length and heading = atan2(vy, vx), st = 1 + min(speed × 0.012, 0.45); the point's local position is (cos θ × r × st, sin θ × r ÷ √st), turned by the heading and added to the blob's position.
- Each frame every blob springs toward its own target point: velocity = (velocity + (target − position) × k × f) × d^f, then position += velocity × f, with k = 0.075, 0.042 and 0.024 and d = 0.8, 0.84 and 0.87 for blobs 0, 1 and 2.
- The targets sit around the aim point on a turning triangle: blob i's target = aim + (cos a, sin a) × R × 0.55, a = t × 0.45 + i × 2π ÷ 3. The aim point is the pointer while it is over the canvas (a finger while it touches); otherwise it wanders: (width × (0.55 + 0.26 × sin(t × 0.47)), height × (0.42 + 0.14 × sin(t × 0.83))).
- Each blob has a scale (starting at 1) on its own spring: scaleVelocity = (scaleVelocity + (1 − scale) × 0.12 × f) × 0.84^f, scale += scaleVelocity × f. A click or tap (on release) makes the blobs jump one after another, blob i after i × 0.07s: when its timer runs out, its scaleVelocity += 0.11.
- Run one requestAnimationFrame loop. dt = seconds since the last frame, clamped to 0.05; f = min(dt × 60, 2.5); t = seconds the loop has run (the sum of dt).
- The blobs start (once, on the first resize) at x = width × (0.48 + i × 0.07), y = height × (0.42 + (i mod 2) × 0.05), at rest, scale 1.
- Reduced-motion still frame: the blobs where they start, overlapping in a trefoil, at t = 0.

## How people interact
- The canvas has touch-action: pan-y, so vertical swipes still scroll.

## Where this goes
First, look at my project's files and figure out what framework and styling it uses. Add this as a component that fits how the project is already built. Don't change unrelated files. When you finish, tell me which files you added or changed and how to put the component on a page.

## Rules
- Smooth on a mid-range laptop and a phone: one requestAnimationFrame loop, batched canvas drawing, devicePixelRatio capped at 2, pause when off-screen.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion: start paused and show a meaningful still frame, with a visible Play/Pause button.
- Accessible: real buttons, visible keyboard focus, text contrast of at least 4.5:1, decorative canvases hidden from screen readers.
- Works on touch screens without blocking vertical scrolling.

## When you're done
Keep the explanation short: list the files and the key parameters I can tweak."
`;

