exports[`pluck prompt > is deterministic and matches its snapshots > detailed-existing-experienced 1`] = `
"# Build: Strings

## What I want
Vertical strings span the screen. Sweeping across them grabs each string in turn; it bends with my pointer until pulled too far, then lets go and rings with a damped vibration. Resting strings are soft gray; ringing ones light up in the accent colors, and in dark worlds they glow. When idle, an invisible hand strums them now and then.

## Look
- Colors ("Aurora" palette): background #06161A, text #E8FFF8, secondary text #9CC9BE, accents #3DF5A8, #6C7BFF, #E66CFF.
- Where accent colors overlap, add them so they glow (canvas globalCompositeOperation "lighter").
- Font: "Anybody" from Google Fonts (variable width 50–150, weight 100–900), loaded with https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Anybody:ital,wdth,wght@0,50..150,100..900;1,50..150,100..900&display=swap, with a system sans-serif fallback.
- Size the canvas for devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and do all math in CSS pixels.

## How it moves
- A full-screen canvas (100svh, at least 540px), transparent, cleared every frame, holds vertical strings gap = clamp(width ÷ 34, 22, 44) px apart: n = max(3, floor((width − gap) ÷ gap)) strings, the first at x = (width − (n − 1) × gap) ÷ 2. Each string is anchored at y = height × 0.12 and y = height × 0.95. String i uses accent (i mod 3).
- In dark worlds (additive blending), a ringing string is stroked four times along its path: in its accent at opacity a × 0.07 and width w × 6, at a × 0.16 and w × 2.8, at a × 0.9 and width w, then in #FFFFFF at a × 0.85 and width max(w × 0.38, 0.6). In light worlds it is stroked once in its accent at opacity a and width w.
- String i's stiffness is 0.03 + 0.08 × (1 − i ÷ (n − 1)), so strings on the left are tighter. A free string's sideways offset rings on a damped spring each frame: velocity = (velocity − stiffness × offset × f) × 0.987^f; offset += velocity × f.
- Pull limit = clamp(gap × 1.9, 42, 84) px. When the pointer (inside the anchored height) crosses a string between two frames ((previous x − string x) × (x − string x) ≤ 0, with x changed), the string is grabbed: its offset follows the pointer (x − string x), its bend point follows the pointer's y (clamped 12px inside the anchors), and its velocity is 0, until the offset passes the pull limit or the pointer leaves; then it rings.
- After 3s without the pointer, an invisible hand sweeps across: direction = random < 0.5 ? left-to-right : right-to-left, y = height × (0.3 + random × 0.3), wave = 0.08 + random × 0.08 (Math.random() in that order). Over 2.1s, u goes from 0 to 1; the hand is at x from −60 to width + 60 (or the reverse) and y + sin(u × 2π × 1.5) × height × wave, and it plucks like the pointer. Then the idle timer restarts. Any real pointer cancels it.
- Draw anchors as dots of radius 2.2px in the secondary text color (one path). For each string, with lit share a = min(|offset| ÷ (pull limit × 0.3), 1) and its path top anchor → (x + offset, bend y) → bottom anchor (round caps and joins): if a < 0.98, stroke it in the secondary text color at opacity (1 − a) × 0.55, width 1.4px; if a > 0.02, stroke it lit with width w = 1.6 + min(|offset| ÷ pull limit, 1) × 3.2 px, using the blend mode above.
- Run one requestAnimationFrame loop. dt = seconds since the last frame, clamped to 0.05; f = min(dt × 60, 2.5).
- Rebuild the strings on every resize; string i keeps its offset and velocity.
- Reduced-motion still frame: the strings as they are (at rest when the page loads).

## How people interact
- Listen for pointer events on the canvas (touch-action: pan-y): a mouse plucks whenever it moves over the canvas, a finger while it touches (sideways drags strum; vertical swipes still scroll).

## Where this goes
First, look at my project's files and figure out what framework and styling it uses. Add this as a component that fits how the project is already built. Don't change unrelated files. When you finish, tell me which files you added or changed and how to put the component on a page.

## Rules
- Smooth on a mid-range laptop and a phone: one requestAnimationFrame loop, batched canvas drawing, devicePixelRatio capped at 2, pause when off-screen.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion: start paused and show a meaningful still frame, with a visible Play/Pause button.
- Accessible: real buttons, visible keyboard focus, text contrast of at least 4.5:1, decorative canvases hidden from screen readers.
- Works on touch screens without blocking vertical scrolling.

## When you're done
Keep the explanation short: list the files and the key parameters I can tweak."
`;

