exports[`rubber prompt > is deterministic and matches its snapshots > detailed-existing-experienced 1`] = `
"# Build: Rubber letters

## What I want
Big, soft letters. When I grab one and pull, it stretches like rubber toward my pull. When I let go it snaps back and wobbles until it settles.

## Look
- Colors ("Lava Lamp" palette): background #240B1C, text #FFEDE3, secondary text #E0AFA0, accents #FF5A1F, #FF2E88, #FFC23D.
- Where accent colors overlap, add them so they glow (canvas globalCompositeOperation "lighter").
- Font: "Anybody" from Google Fonts (variable width 50–150, weight 100–900), loaded with https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Anybody:ital,wdth,wght@0,50..150,100..900;1,50..150,100..900&display=swap, with a system sans-serif fallback.
- A full-screen canvas (100svh, at least 540px), transparent, cleared every frame. Size it for devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and do all math in CSS pixels.

## How it moves
- The word is "rubber" in Anybody weight 900, canvas fontStretch "expanded", one centered line at 48% of the height; font size = height × 0.3, scaled down if wider than 82% of the width. Each letter is its own soft piece, filled in accent (letter index mod 3), drawn with textAlign center and textBaseline middle at its center (where it sits in the line, measured from the text before it, plus half its width). Spaces are skipped.
- Each letter has a stretch vector V (in px, starting at 0). Draw it as: translate(center + V / 2), rotate(θ), scale(1 + |V| ÷ font size, (1 + |V| ÷ font size)^−0.5), rotate(−θ), where θ is V's angle; so it lengthens toward the pull, one end staying put, and thins out sideways. While a letter is held, V eases toward (pointer − where it was grabbed) × 1, by 0.35 × f of the way each frame, never longer than 2.5 × the font size.
- Released letters spring back: velocity = (velocity − V × 0.2 × f) × 0.9^f; V += velocity × f, so they overshoot and wobble until they settle.
- Run one requestAnimationFrame loop. dt = seconds since the last frame, clamped to 0.05; f = min(dt × 60, 2.5).
- Lay the word out again on every resize and once the Anybody font has finished loading.
- Reduced-motion still frame: every letter at rest except the middle one (index floor(count ÷ 2)), caught mid-wobble with V pointing straight up at 0.35 × max stretch × font size.

## How people interact
- Press on a letter (inside its box) to grab it: a mouse at once, a finger after holding still for 0.25s, so swipes still scroll (touch-action: pan-y). Release lets go. Draw the held letter last.

## Where this goes
First, look at my project's files and figure out what framework and styling it uses. Add this as a component that fits how the project is already built. Don't change unrelated files. When you finish, tell me which files you added or changed and how to put the component on a page.

## Rules
- Smooth on a mid-range laptop and a phone: one requestAnimationFrame loop, batched canvas drawing, devicePixelRatio capped at 2, pause when off-screen.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion: start paused and show a meaningful still frame, with a visible Play/Pause button.
- Accessible: real buttons, visible keyboard focus, text contrast of at least 4.5:1, decorative canvases hidden from screen readers.
- Works on touch screens without blocking vertical scrolling.

## When you're done
Keep the explanation short: list the files and the key parameters I can tweak."
`;

